  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 153 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nA cold front passing through late Friday night to possibly early \nSaturday morning will bring a nice reprieve from the hot weather. \nDaytime highs Saturday are expected to be in the mid to upper 70s \nacross western and north central Nebraska. a deep upper level low \nwill make landfall onto the Pacific Northwest sometime early Sunday \nmorning. This will dig into what was generally zonal upper flow and \ncreating a ridging pattern ahead, over the Northern Plains. The \nriding will allow high pressure to settle into Nebraska and promote \nwarmer temperatures with southwest flow. Sunday highs are expected \nto be in the mid to uppers 80s across southwest Nebraska and in the \nmid to upper 70s across North Central. The southwest flow aloft\nwill also bring an influx of moisture from the eastern Pacific.\nPWATs are expected to climb to over 1.00\" and central to \neastern Nebraska could see that rise to 1.5\". This would put the\narea in well over the 95th percentile climatologically for the \nyear. As such, the prospect of widespread wetting rainfall \nremains possible. NBM remains fairly aggressive with 40 to 50% \nprobabilities of areas north of I80 and along/west of Highway 83\nseeing >0.25\" rainfall Sunday through Monday morning. LREF is a\nbit more bullish with the same area seeing 20 to 40% \nprobabilities. Regardless, with mean MUCAPE being 1,000 to 2,000\nJ/kg, there remains the possibility for rainfall to come in the\nway of strong to potentially severe thunderstorms. This \npossibility will be something to watch for in the coming days as\nthe forecast continues to be refined. \n\nAs the upper trough from the Pacific Northwest traverses past the \nRockies and reaches the northern Plains around Monday afternoon, \ndaytime highs next week will feel \"fall like\". Daytime highs are \nexpected to be in the mid to upper 70s Monday through Wednesday for \nthe CWA. To accompany the cooler temperatures will be mostly dry \nforecast until mid week where a shortwave could further bring some \nprecipitation potential.",
